on” proxy) The pyloric opening is a physical filter. It only allows liquid containing tiny particles (under about 2 mm) to pass. Large solid chunks physically can’t get through and get bounced back (retropulsion). Because older food has been exposed to acid and grinding longer, it is more likely to be reduced to the required small particle size. New food is mostly in large pieces and would be rejected. So the pylorus doesn’t need a temporal tag; it just mechanically excludes large particles—and those happen to be the fresh ones. The size threshold acts as a de facto age discriminator. ### 4. Liquid newly drunk can bypass the queue, but that’s a special case If you drink water or a clear liquid soon after eating solids, that liquid can slide along the lesser curvature (the Magenstrasse) and reach the pylorus quickly, essentially “cutting in line.” This demonstrates that the system doesn’t enforce a strict FIFO based on time—it differentiates based on *physical state*, not chronology. However, for solids, the three mechanisms above do create a robust FIFO-like order, which is why your real-world observation holds: meals generally empty in the order they were eaten, especially if they’re solid. ### 5. Hormonal and neural feedback refines the gradient When small amounts of chyme finally do exit, the duodenum senses its acidity, fat content, and osmolarity, and can signal the stomach to slow emptying (enterogastric reflex). This prevents overload. It doesn’t distinguish old from new directly, but it helps pace the outflow so that the stomach doesn’t just dump everything at once, giving even more time for newer solids to get broken down while older chyme continues to be released in small spurts. --- ### So, in direct answer to your original, clarified question: The stomach differentiates newer food from older food by: - **Where it sits** (new on top/in the fundus; old at the bottom/in the antrum), - **Whether it’s getting ground** (antral contractions act on the lower, ol